      "updated": 1788379057849,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "11) Identify slow GPU in distributed training",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "11) Identify slow GPU in distributed training",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wFecDiE25122Zq7HkIwjd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 196.26886250579707,
      "y": 8877.28355928421,
      "width": 1236.4999600026224,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2A",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1105491463,
      "version": 288,
      "versionNonce": 76107721,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788379076950,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "wouldn’t identify a straggler from GPU utilization alone. I’d instrument every rank’s step phases and synchronization arrival\ntimes. The straggler is typically the rank that consistently arrives last at collective operations; the other ranks’ elevated all-\nreduce time is often waiting, not a network problem. Then I’d correlate that rank with SM utilization, clocks, thermals,\nNVLink/PCIe, H2D latency, dataloader time, and batch characteristics to determine whether the root cause is GPU hardware,\ncommunication, input pipeline, or workload imbalance",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "wouldn’t identify a straggler from GPU utilization alone. I’d instrument every rank’s step phases and synchronization arrival times. The straggler is typically the rank that consistently arrives last at collective operations; the other ranks’ elevated all-reduce time is often waiting, not a network problem. Then I’d correlate that rank with SM utilization, clocks, thermals, NVLink/PCIe, H2D latency, dataloader time, and batch characteristics to determine whether the root cause is GPU hardware, communication, input pipeline, or workload imbalance",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "BvaNPoy-5Ag2CTj66zCJo",
